<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hercynian Forest</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hercynia Silva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hercynian Forest was the name given by Greek and Roman writers to the great forest of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, stretching east from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> wrote that it took nine days to cross. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> says the Celtic leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovesus">Segovesus</a> led migrants into it, and its name survives in the geological term Hercynian.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The forest's boundaries are a matter of debate, and the ancient sources are equivocal about how far east it extended. It formed the northern boundary of the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> known to writers of antiquity. Many took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley, as its western side, though Lucius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a> included massifs west of the river. Across the Rhine lay the Silva Carbonaria and the forests of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, all part of Europe's original post-glacial temperate broadleaf forest.</p><p>The name has a Proto-Celtic derivation, from erkunia; Julius Pokorny traced it to a word for oak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, the earliest writer to mention it, says only that rivers flow north from its mountains, and the form Hercynia first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Caesar's time the forest blocked the advance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>. In De Bello Gallico he describes it as running along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> from the territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, its eastern edge more than sixty days' march away, and repeats old tales of unicorns; the passages on elk without joints that leaned against trees to sleep were probably later additions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> placed its eastern parts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and Dacia and wrote of gigantic oaks and of birds whose feathers shine like fires at night.</p><p>The forest hindered the last concerted Roman foray into it, by Drusus in 12–9 BCE. Its animals included reindeer, elk, the European bison and the now-extinct aurochs. Before the Boii the Hercuniates are believed to have lived there; they later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and issued their own coins by the second century BCE. Monks sent out from Niederaltaich Abbey, founded in the eighth century, cleared great areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a> and founded 120 settlements in what came to be known as the Bavarian Forest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Relict tracts of the forest survive under many local names, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>, the Bavarian Forest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a> and the Bohemian Forest. Some geographers apply the name Hercynian Forest to the complex of mountain ranges and plateaus that stretches from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> across Middle Germany and along the northern borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hercynian Forest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Marcomanni then moved, under their new king Maroboduus, who had grown up in Rome, into Boiohaemum, plains surrounded by the <b>Hercynian Forest</b> where the Boii had lived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Livy tells a legend that in the 6th century BCE their king Ambigatus held supremacy over the Celts and sent his nephews Bellovesus and Segovesus to lead migrations into Italy and the <b>Hercynian Forest</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-cubi">Bituriges Cubi</a></li></ul></section>
